# PHASE 1 — STABILIZATION AND CRITICAL FIXES

Complete this phase before implementing optional enhancements.

## 1.1 Fix MediaProjection and VirtualDisplay lifecycle

Primary file: `app/src/main/java/com/gala/motetv/mirror/ScreenMirrorService.kt`

Implement a reliable screen-capture lifecycle that complies with Android 14+ MediaProjection restrictions.

Requirements:

* Register `MediaProjection.Callback` before the first `createVirtualDisplay()` call.
* Create exactly one `VirtualDisplay` per MediaProjection session.
* On rotation or display metric changes, update the existing display with `VirtualDisplay.resize()` and `VirtualDisplay.setSurface()`.
* Never create a second virtual display using the same projection session.
* Create a correctly sized `ImageReader` for the new dimensions.
* Replace the surface safely without leaving old readers or surfaces attached.
* Close the old reader only after the existing display has been switched to the new surface.
* Handle rapid rotation, duplicate display-change callbacks, and shutdown during resizing.
* Keep all pipeline mutations protected by a consistent synchronization strategy.
* Prevent callbacks from a replaced ImageReader from publishing stale frames.
* Ensure all display listeners and projection callbacks are unregistered during teardown.
* Ensure that a new session can start after the previous session stops or fails.

Use an explicit lifecycle state, such as `IDLE`, `STARTING`, `ACTIVE`, and `STOPPING`, or an equally robust existing mechanism.

Make start and stop operations idempotent. Do not let a Boolean flag cause resource cleanup to be skipped.

## 1.2 Fix partial-startup cleanup

Currently, the HTTP server may start before MediaProjection initialization completes.

Implement cleanup so that if any step fails, all previously allocated resources are released.

Clean up:

* HTTP server and client sockets.
* Session token and published stream URLs.
* MediaProjection callback and projection.
* VirtualDisplay.
* ImageReader.
* Display listener.
* Cached bitmaps.
* Reusable buffers.
* Pending frame-processing work and related service resources.

Test failures at each initialization stage. Verify that a failed start does not leave an active server, stale token, foreground notification, or unusable state.

Repeated stop requests must not crash, decrement counters twice, or prevent a later successful start.

## 1.3 Guarantee ImageReader frame cleanup

For every acquired `Image`:

* Close it in a `finally` block.
* Close it safely if pixel copying or bitmap processing throws.
* Reset the frame-processing busy flag in its own `finally` block.
* Never queue an unbounded number of frames.
* Drop incoming frames when compression is busy.
* Prevent stale callbacks from touching recycled bitmaps.
* Keep the image acquisition and bitmap ownership rules explicit.

Reuse buffers only when their ownership and concurrent access are safe. Review `ByteArrayOutputStream.toByteArray()`, which still copies the encoded bytes. Do not claim zero allocations unless measured and demonstrated.

## 1.4 Fix server lifecycle and viewer-count races

Primary file: `app/src/main/java/com/gala/motetv/mirror/ScreenMirrorServer.kt`

Requirements:

* Each client connection must increment the viewer count at most once.
* Each registered client must decrement it at most once.
* Disconnect, socket failure, server shutdown, and repeated cleanup must not cause negative or inaccurate counts.
* Server shutdown must invalidate the token immediately and close client sockets without blocking the main thread.
* Do not reset the count independently of active client cleanup in a way that permits subsequent decrements to corrupt the count.
* Prevent stale worker threads from publishing state after shutdown.
* Make start/stop and port binding thread-safe.
* Preserve port fallback behavior and return the actual bound port.
* Reject loopback, link-local, and otherwise unusable LAN addresses when generating a URL for a TV.
* Show a useful error when the phone has no suitable LAN address.

Preserve authenticated streaming. Never log session tokens or complete tokenized URLs.

## 1.5 Make release signing safe

File: `app/build.gradle.kts`

Correct the release signing configuration.

* `ALLOW_DEBUG_RELEASE_SIGNING` must default to `false`.
* A missing production keystore must never silently trigger debug signing.
* Production release builds must require the intended keystore and valid credentials.
* Validate `KEYSTORE_PATH`, `STORE_PASSWORD`, `KEY_ALIAS`, and `KEY_PASSWORD`.
* Support an explicit local/test-only debug-signing opt-in when production signing is not required.
* If `RELEASE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=true`, reject debug fallback regardless of other configuration.
* Fail early with a clear actionable error.
* Preserve ordinary debug builds.
* Document how to inspect the signing certificate of an APK or AAB.
* Never commit real keystores, passwords, tokens, or signing secrets.

Test both the production-required and explicitly opted-in local/test scenarios.

## 1.6 Preserve and improve casting security

Review the existing media server and mirroring server.

* Use short-lived, unpredictable session tokens.
* Invalidate tokens on stop and failed startup.
* Validate token authorization on every new stream/viewer request.
* Do not expose tokens in application logs.
* Restrict the service to intended local-network use.
* Avoid unnecessarily exposing the streaming server beyond the local network.
