## 6.2 Adaptive algorithm

Implement a bounded, deterministic algorithm based on actual observations.

Possible signals:

* Measured frame encoding time.
* Actual frames delivered per second.
* Dropped-frame count.
* Number of active viewers.
* Stream write failures or sustained slow-client behavior.
* Current configured dimensions and JPEG quality.

Do not use arbitrary invented bandwidth measurements. If actual throughput is unavailable, do not label an estimate as measured network bandwidth.

Suggested behaviour:

* When encoding time remains high or dropped frames persist, lower JPEG quality or capture resolution one step.
* When performance remains healthy for a sustained interval, gradually restore quality.
* Apply cooldowns and hysteresis to prevent settings from oscillating.
* Keep the user's manual settings when automatic mode is disabled.
* Avoid changing display metrics unnecessarily.
* Avoid resizing the VirtualDisplay unless capture dimensions genuinely need to change.
* Maintain the single-VirtualDisplay lifecycle rules from Phase 1.

Expose current mode and any automatic quality changes in the UI.

## 6.3 Streaming efficiency

* Maintain bounded memory use.
* Avoid blocking MediaProjection callbacks on slow socket writes.
* Ensure one slow client cannot stall capture or all other clients.
* Reuse buffers only with correct synchronization and ownership.
* Measure performance before and after optimisations.
* Add diagnostic counters for encoded frames, dropped frames, encoding time, and viewer failures.
* Reset session counters when a new mirroring session starts.

Do not promise a specific FPS or latency without measurements.

